==============================================================================
(0) THE PARITY OBSTRUCTION:  sum of scores = C(n,2)
==============================================================================
   n   n mod 4   C(n,2)   C(n,2) parity   all-even tournament possible?
   3      3         3       ODD             NO -- impossible
   4      0         6       even            YES
   5      1        10       even            YES
   6      2        15       ODD             NO -- impossible
   7      3        21       ODD             NO -- impossible
   8      0        28       even            YES
   9      1        36       even            YES
  10      2        45       ODD             NO -- impossible
  11      3        55       ODD             NO -- impossible
  12      0        66       even            YES
  13      1        78       even            YES

==============================================================================
(1) EXHAUSTIVE n = 5:  does every switching class contain exactly ONE all-even?
==============================================================================
   labelled tournaments: 1024
   switching classes:    64   (expected 2^C(n-1,2) = 64)
   all-even members per switching class: {1: 64}
   => UNIQUE even member in every class -- CONFIRMED

==============================================================================
(2) n = 9 (also 1 mod 4), by sampling switching classes
==============================================================================
   all-even members per switching class over 60 random classes: {1: 60}
   => UNIQUE -- CONFIRMED at n=9

==============================================================================
(3) n = 3, 7 (= 3 mod 4): verify NO all-even tournament exists
==============================================================================
   n=3: all-even tournaments found = 0  (C(n,2)=3 is odd, so 0 is forced)
   n=7: all-even tournaments found = 0  (C(n,2)=21 is odd, so 0 is forced)

==============================================================================
(4) CONCLUSION FOR BABAI-CAMERON REMARK 7.4
==============================================================================
   If each switching class at n = 1 (mod 4) has a UNIQUE all-even member E, then any
   automorphism g of the switching class permutes the class and preserves the
   all-even property (relabelling does not change the multiset of scores), so g(E) is
   an all-even member, hence g(E) = E by uniqueness.  So g FIXES a member.
   => the number of switching classes with an automorphism fixing NO member is ZERO
      at n = 1 (mod 4).  Babai-Cameron's 'we cannot do this' has a clean answer on
      that residue class.
   At n = 3 (mod 4) there is NO even member to anchor the argument -- which is exactly
      where the enumeration stays hard, and exactly where Paley TOURNAMENTS live.
